TITLE:: MKtlElementMenu
summary:: right-click context menu for MKtlGUI element views
categories:: Libraries>Modality, External Control
related:: Classes/MKtlGUI, Classes/MKtl, Classes/MAbstractElement

DESCRIPTION::

MKtlElementMenu adds a right-click context menu to every element view in an
link::Classes/MKtlGUI::. It is wired up automatically, so there is nothing to call:

code::
MKtl('grid', "intech-grid-nesso").gui;   // right-click any knob, fader, button or pad
::

The menu tells you how to address the clicked element in code, copies
ready-to-paste action templates to the clipboard, shows every action currently
registered on it, and posts its full description.

Right-click is swallowed, so it never also moves the widget or fires its action.
On macOS Qt delivers ctrl+click as a right-click, so trackpads work too.

subsection:: Menu entries

definitionlist::
## header || the element's code::elAt:: path, e.g. code::MKtl('grid').elAt(\knobModule, 3, 0)::
## Copy > action_ template || a bare skeleton, ready to fill in
## Copy > addAction template || the same, but appending rather than replacing
## Copy > element path || just the path
## Copy > group action || one entry per ancestor group, in the code::{ |el, grp| }:: form
## Copy > .kr snippet || for use in a SynthDef, via link::Classes/MAbstractElement#-kr::
## Show actions in new document || every action that fires for the element, as re-evaluable code
## Post element info || name, type, ioType, spec, value, tags, and the protocol detail from code::elemDesc::
::

Where a code::groupType:: group makes several elements share one underlying view,
the menu shows one submenu per element rather than letting all but one become
unreachable.

subsection:: Element methods

The code-string helpers are added to link::Classes/MAbstractElement::, so they
work on any element or group from the post window, with no GUI involved:

code::
k = MKtl('grid', "intech-grid-nesso");

k.elAt(\knobModule, 3, 0).elemPath;          // -> [ knobModule, 3, 0 ]
k.elAt(\knobModule, 3, 0).elemPathString;    // -> "MKtl('grid').elAt(\knobModule, 3, 0)"
k.elAt(\knobModule, 3, 0).elemPathArgString; // -> "\knobModule, 3, 0"
k.elAt(\knobModule, 3, 0).actionTemplateString;
k.elAt(\knobModule, 3, 0).actionTemplateString(\addAction);
k.elAt(\knobModule, 3, 0).krCodeString;
k.elAt(\knobModule, 3, 0).actionsAsString;
k.elAt(\knobModule, 3, 0).postElemInfo;

// and on String:
"anything".copyToClipboard;
::

code::elemPath:: walks the code::parent:: chain and picks, at each level, the
group key when it is a real name and the integer index when
link::Classes/MKtlDesc:: only filled in a positional code::(i+1).asSymbol::.
That is what produces code::elAt(\knobModule, 3, 0):: rather than
code::elAt(\knobModule, '4', '1')::. It round-trips:

code::
k.elementGroup.flat.every { |el| k.elAt(*el.elemPath) === el };   // -> true
::

note::
link::Classes/MAbstractElement#-keys:: looks like it does the same thing, but it
does not - it recurses into code::MKtlElementGroup:keys::, which returns a
group's strong::child:: keys rather than its ancestor path. This quark does not
touch it.
::

subsection:: Methods overwritten in Modality

Nothing in Modality-toolkit is modified, but two of its methods are overwritten
from this quark. code::MethodOverride.printAll:: should list exactly these two:

definitionlist::
## code::MKtlGUI:*new:: || one line reproduced verbatim from upstream, plus the call to code::addContextMenus::. Hooking code::*new:: rather than code::MKtl:gui:: covers both entry points, and avoids extension-overwrites-extension, which resolves by compile order.
## code::MKtlElementView:snapback_:: || reimplemented as flag-only. Upstream it installs and removes code::view.mouseDownAction:: directly, which would blow away the menu wrapper whenever code::t:: or code::m:: is pressed over a widget. The click behaviour moves into code::MKtlGUI:addContextMenus:: instead, where it also skips button 1.
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: qtViewOf
Follow code::.view:: until the real link::Classes/View:: underneath is reached.
Handles all the shapes code::MKtlElementView:view:: can take: plain Views,
link::Classes/MPadView::-style code::SCViewHolder::s, and the
code::MPadUpViewRedirect:: spoof objects.
argument:: obj
returns:: a View, or nil

METHOD:: attachTo
Install the menu on a view, wrapping rather than replacing its existing mouse
handlers so that code::MPadView::'s own actions keep working.
argument:: qtView
argument:: elementViews
the MKtlElementViews sharing this view

METHOD:: showMenuFor
Build a fresh menu and pop it up at the cursor.
argument:: elementViews

METHOD:: menuFor
argument:: elements
returns:: a link::Classes/Menu::

METHOD:: actionsFor, copyMenuFor
build the link::Classes/MenuAction::s for one element
argument:: element

METHOD:: ancestorGroupsOf
every ancestor group that has a usable code::elAt:: path
argument:: element

METHOD:: currentMenu
The menu currently on screen. code::QMenu::popup:: is non-modal, so it is held
here to keep it from being collected while it is showing.

private:: prCopy

INSTANCEMETHODS::

EXAMPLES::

code::
k = MKtl('grid', "intech-grid-nesso");
g = k.gui;

// right-click the fourth knob in the last row, choose Copy > action_ template,
// paste, and fill in:
MKtl('grid').elAt(\knobModule, 3, 0).action_({ |el|
	el.value.postln;
});

// right-click it again and choose "Show actions in new document"
::
